<div id="serial-panel" style="display: none;">
  <div class="oscar-serial">
    <p class="oscar-serial-hint">
      OSCAR can send to a board on a USB cable as well as over the network.
      Connect the port below, then set a widget's <b>Ip</b> to
      <code>serial</code> — its Port is ignored. Messages go out as OSC framed
      with SLIP, which is what the Arduino OSC libraries read.
    </p>

    <div id="serial-status" class="oscar-serial-status">Checking…</div>

    <div class="oscar-serial-controls">
      <select id="serial-port" class="form-control"></select>
      <input
        id="serial-bitrate"
        class="form-control oscar-serial-bitrate"
        type="number"
        min="300"
        max="2000000"
        value="115200"
        title="Baud rate — match Serial.begin in your sketch"
      />
    </div>

    <div class="oscar-serial-actions">
      <button id="serial-refresh" class="btn btn-secondary" type="button">Refresh</button>
      <button id="serial-connect" class="btn btn-secondary btn-gradient" type="button">
        Connect
      </button>
      <button id="serial-disconnect" class="btn btn-secondary" type="button">Disconnect</button>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
